          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, means that the pressure in your arteries is persistently raised over time, not just on a single occasion. Blood pressure is recorded as two numbers: the systolic pressure (when the heart beats) over the diastolic pressure (between beats) (condition-hypertension).\n\nA single high blood pressure reading is different from hypertension because blood pressure naturally changes throughout the day due to activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep, and time of day. Therefore, a diagnosis of high blood pressure requires repeated measurements rather than just one reading. A single high reading in a clinic does not mean you have high blood pressure (condition-hypertension).\n\nFrom a classification perspective, a single elevated reading is considered a \"finding\" and not a disease. A diagnosis of hypertension, which is documented in your medical record, is what formally classifies it as high blood pressure (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
